Exactly. “Common sense” is often a compressed model of conditions that already worked somewhere. Strip away the hidden assumptions, and the apparently obvious move may stop being obvious—or even feasible.

The Irish Famine is a strong example. “Why didn’t they just fish?” sounds almost absurdly straightforward: Ireland is an island, surrounded by protein. But the relevant question is not “Were there fish?” It is “Could millions of desperately poor people convert nearby fish into dependable calories at famine scale?”

Many could not. Fishing required boats, nets, hooks, sails, maintenance, knowledge, access to harbors, and enough physical reserves to work at sea. Much of the rural poor had almost no capital because the potato had enabled families to survive on tiny rented plots. When the crop failed, they weren’t sitting on spare assets with which to bootstrap a commercial fishing fleet. In many cases people were selling or pawning possessions just to buy food.

And Ireland absolutely did have fisheries. People fished before and during the Great Famine. The problem was that fishing was not remotely large or productive enough to replace a staple crop feeding millions. Potatoes were extraordinarily efficient at producing calories per acre, could be grown close to the household, stored reasonably well, and required no transportation network between ocean and inland population. Fish are comparatively perishable; nineteenth-century refrigeration did not exist, preservation required salt or curing infrastructure, and transporting massive quantities inland required functioning roads, markets, carts, capital, and purchasing power.

There was another nasty feedback loop: famine destroys the capacity to solve famine. Malnutrition weakens fishermen. Poverty causes people to sell nets and boats. Credit disappears. Markets contract because customers have no money. Skilled labor emigrates. Disease increases. Transportation becomes harder. So the solution space actually shrinks as the crisis intensifies.

Then layer the political economy on top. Ireland was not an isolated subsistence island collectively deciding how to allocate its resources. Landownership, tenancy, rents, commercial agriculture, British policy, poor-relief rules, food prices, exports, and private property all constrained who could command food and productive assets. Other food continued to be produced in Ireland while people starved because the existence of food and access to food are different variables.

So the retrospective sentence:

“They should have fished.”

quietly assumes something like:

ocean + fish = food

But the operational equation is closer to:

fish × boats × gear × skill × weather × health × capital × property access × preservation × transport × distribution × purchasing power × time = calories actually reaching hungry people

If any of several terms approaches zero, the intuitive answer collapses.

And that gets directly at your larger point. Hindsight erases dependency graphs. Once somebody shows you the successful path, your brain compresses the path into a single move and calls it obvious. Before the fact, the move existed inside a huge branching tree of constraints, unknowns, costs, institutions, and competing choices.

So “common sense” is useful as a heuristic layer, but it is a terrible bedrock epistemology. A better question is often:

“What invisible assumptions must be true for this obvious solution to work?”

That is where a lot of supposedly stupid historical behavior suddenly becomes intelligible—not necessarily correct, but intelligible.